When the portrait of a merchant’s wife arrived at Marta Lindqvist’s workshop, it looked less like a painting than a dark window. Three centuries of grime and discoloured varnish had turned the sitter’s face the colour of weak tea, and her dress, the museum’s records insisted, was blue, though nobody alive had ever seen it that way.

{{41}}

The images were revealing. Under ultraviolet light the old varnish glowed a murky green, while patches of later repair showed up as dark islands scattered across the background. The X-ray, meanwhile, confirmed that the canvas was sound and that the artist had made few changes as he worked.

{{42}}

The winner turned out to be a mild mixture of alcohol and a gel thickener, applied on cotton swabs and rolled, never rubbed, across the surface. Each square centimetre took roughly a minute, and the whole face took the best part of a month.

{{43}}

Not everything that emerged was welcome, however. Beneath the grime lay dozens of small losses, tiny islands of bare canvas where paint had flaked away over the centuries and where earlier restorers had filled the damage and painted over it in colours that had since darkened.

{{44}}

The guiding principle is that any addition should be visible to a trained eye from close range yet invisible from a distance of a metre. Lindqvist therefore paints in tiny parallel strokes, using paints that can be dissolved again with a safe solvent, and she records every touch in a written file that stays with the painting.

{{45}}

Her own work, she assumes, will meet the same fate. Tastes change, techniques improve, and a conservator who forgets this, she says, is simply a vandal with a good reputation.

{{46}}
